window.ND=window.ND||{};Object.assign(ND,{"ip|Temperature regulation":{"d":"## Body temperature\n- **Core temperature ≈ 37 °C (36.5–37.5)**. Normal **oral ≈ 36.8 ± 0.4 °C (98.2–98.6 °F)**; **rectal ≈ 0.5 °C higher** than oral; axillary ≈ 0.5 °C lower.\n- Shell (skin) temperature varies with the environment.\n- **Circadian variation ≈ 0.5–1 °C**: lowest in the early morning (≈ 4–6 am), highest in the evening.\n- In women the temperature **rises ≈ 0.3–0.5 °C after ovulation** (progesterone), the basis of the **BBT method**.\n- Higher in exercise, after meals and in infants.\n\n## Heat production\n- Basal metabolism (liver, heart, brain at rest; muscle in exercise).\n- Muscular activity and **shivering**, which can raise heat production 4–5×.\n- Thermic effect of food.\n- Hormones: thyroxine (slow), adrenaline and noradrenaline (non-shivering thermogenesis).\n- **Brown adipose tissue** (UCP-1/thermogenin uncouples oxidative phosphorylation). Important in **neonates** (interscapular, around the kidneys).\n\n## Heat loss\n- At rest in a cool environment:\n  - **Radiation ≈ 60%**\n  - **Evaporation ≈ 22%**: insensible ≈ 600–700 mL/day from skin and lungs; sweat\n  - Conduction to air and convection ≈ 15%\n  - Conduction to objects ≈ 3%\n- **When environmental temperature exceeds skin temperature, evaporation is the only route of heat loss.** Each gram of water evaporated removes ≈ 0.58 kcal.\n- Evaporation fails in high humidity (relevant to humid Indian summers; wet-bulb temperature).\n- **Sweating**: eccrine glands, **sympathetic cholinergic** (ACh, muscarinic) innervation. Up to 1–2 L/h in acclimatised people. Hypotonic sweat (aldosterone conserves Na⁺ with acclimatisation).\n- **Skin blood flow**: arteriovenous anastomoses in the hands, feet and ears; cutaneous vasodilatation can raise skin flow to ≈ 7–8 L/min (≈ 30% of cardiac output).\n\n## Regulation (thermostat)\n**Hypothalamic set point ≈ 37 °C.**\n- **Anterior hypothalamus/preoptic area**: heat-sensitive neurons. It is the **heat-loss centre**: vasodilatation, sweating, panting. A lesion causes hyperthermia.\n- **Posterior hypothalamus**: integrates cold signals from skin cold receptors. It is the **heat-gain (conservation) centre**: vasoconstriction, shivering (primary motor centre for shivering), piloerection, ↑thyroxine (TRH) and catecholamines. A lesion causes poikilothermia.\n- Inputs: central thermoreceptors (hypothalamus, spinal cord, viscera) and peripheral (skin cold receptors outnumber warm ones).\n- Behavioural responses: clothing, shelter, posture.\n\n## Fever (pyrexia)\n1. Exogenous pyrogens (LPS) act on macrophages.\n2. Macrophages release **endogenous pyrogens**: **IL-1, IL-6, TNF-α**, interferons.\n3. These act at the **OVLT** (circumventricular organ).\n4. **COX-2** is induced, producing **PGE₂**.\n5. PGE₂ acts on EP3 receptors and **raises the set point**.\n\nCourse:\n- **Chills** (shivering, cold skin) while the body temperature climbs to the new set point.\n- **Crisis/flush** (sweating, vasodilatation) when the set point falls.\n- Antipyretics (paracetamol, NSAIDs) inhibit COX.\n- Patterns: continuous, remittent, intermittent (malaria: tertian/quartan; typhoid: step-ladder; dengue: saddleback), all relevant in India.\n\n## Heat disorders\n- **Heat cramps**: salt loss.\n- **Heat exhaustion**: water/salt depletion, hypotension, core < 40 °C.\n- **Heat stroke**: **core > 40–40.5 °C** with CNS dysfunction and often **hot, dry skin** (thermoregulatory failure). Multi-organ failure. **Emergency rapid cooling** (cold-water immersion, evaporative cooling). Indian heat waves cause major mortality.\n- **Malignant hyperthermia**: RyR1 mutation, triggered by halothane/succinylcholine. Treated with **dantrolene**.\n- **Acclimatisation to heat** takes ≈ 1–2 weeks: sweating starts earlier and is more profuse, salt content falls, plasma volume rises and heart rate falls.\n\n## Cold disorders\n- **Hypothermia**: core **< 35 °C**. Mild 32–35, moderate 28–32, severe < 28 °C (risk of VF, J/Osborn waves on ECG). Below ≈ 30 °C the regulatory ability is lost (\"not dead until warm and dead\").\n- Frostbite (Siachen, high altitude); trench foot.\n- **Therapeutic hypothermia** (32–36 °C) after cardiac arrest and in cardiac surgery.\n- Neonates and the elderly are prone to hypothermia. Kangaroo mother care is practised in India.","q":{"b":["Oral 36.8 ± 0.4 °C; rectal ≈ 0.5 °C higher.","Lowest early morning, highest evening.","Post-ovulatory rise 0.3–0.5 °C (progesterone).","At rest radiation ≈ 60% of heat loss.","Above skin temperature, evaporation is the only route.","Sweat glands – sympathetic cholinergic.","Anterior hypothalamus – heat loss.","Posterior hypothalamus – heat gain, shivering.","Fever: IL-1, IL-6, TNF → PGE₂ → ↑set point.","Brown fat (UCP-1) – neonatal thermogenesis.","Heat stroke > 40 °C with CNS dysfunction, hot dry skin.","Hypothermia < 35 °C.","Malignant hyperthermia – dantrolene."],"n":["Core 37 °C","Diurnal 0.5–1 °C","Heat stroke > 40 °C","Hypothermia < 35 °C","0.58 kcal/g evaporated"],"m":"**\"Anterior = A/C (cooling), Posterior = Put on a sweater (heating)\"**.","v":["Mediator of fever acting on the hypothalamus? PGE₂.","Only heat-loss route when air is hotter than skin? Evaporation.","Transmitter at sweat glands? Acetylcholine."]}},"ip|Exercise":{"d":"## Types\n- **Dynamic (isotonic, aerobic)**: running, swimming. Large volume load; systolic BP rises, diastolic stays the same or falls.\n- **Static (isometric)**: weightlifting, handgrip. Pressure load; **both systolic and diastolic rise markedly**.\n- By intensity, based on **VO₂max** (maximal O₂ uptake): mild < 40%, moderate 40–60%, severe > 60–75%.\n- **VO₂max** ≈ 35–45 mL/kg/min in untrained young men (lower in women), up to 70–85 in elite endurance athletes. It is the best index of aerobic fitness and is limited mainly by cardiac output.\n\n## Energy systems\n1. **ATP–creatine phosphate (phosphagen)**: first ≈ 10 s (sprint).\n2. **Anaerobic glycolysis**: lactate, ≈ 1–2 min.\n3. **Aerobic oxidation**: carbohydrate → fat as duration increases.\n\nRelated measures:\n- **Lactate (anaerobic) threshold**: the intensity at which blood lactate begins to rise sharply, ≈ 50–60% VO₂max in untrained people, higher (≈ 70–80%) when trained.\n- **Respiratory exchange ratio** rises towards and above 1.0 during heavy exercise (bicarbonate buffering of lactate).\n- **Oxygen debt (EPOC)**: extra O₂ consumed after exercise to resynthesise ATP/CP, remove lactate, restore O₂ stores (myoglobin, Hb) and cover raised temperature and catecholamines. Can be up to ≈ 10–20 L after severe exercise.\n- **Oxygen deficit** is the lag at the start of exercise.\n\n## Cardiovascular responses\n- **Cardiac output** rises from 5 to **≈ 20–25 L/min** (up to 35–40 L/min in elite athletes).\n  - **Heart rate** rises linearly with workload, up to a maximum ≈ **220 − age**.\n  - **Stroke volume** rises from 70 to ≈ 100–120 mL (plateaus at ≈ 40–50% VO₂max) through the muscle pump, ↑venous return and sympathetic inotropy.\n- **Muscle blood flow** rises up to ≈ 20× (≈ 0.75 → 15–20 L/min), from metabolic vasodilatation (K⁺, adenosine, H⁺, CO₂, lactate, ↓O₂, NO) and capillary recruitment.\n- Vasoconstriction in the splanchnic and renal beds (and skin initially; later skin dilates for heat loss). **Coronary flow rises**; cerebral flow is roughly constant.\n- **BP**: systolic rises (up to 160–200 mmHg); diastolic same or slightly lower in dynamic exercise because of ↓TPR. Mean pressure rises modestly.\n- **Central command** (the motor cortex irradiates the medullary centres) gives an anticipatory rise. Muscle **mechanoreceptor and metaboreceptor (exercise pressor) reflexes** and baroreceptor resetting also act.\n- **a–vO₂ difference** widens from 5 to ≈ 15 mL/dL (more extraction; right shift of the ODC from ↑temperature, CO₂, H⁺, 2,3-BPG).\n\n## Respiratory responses\n- Ventilation rises from ≈ 6 L/min to **100–150 L/min** (tidal volume first, then rate).\n- An **abrupt rise at onset** (neural: central command, joint/muscle proprioceptors), then a gradual rise (temperature, K⁺, catecholamines, chemoreceptor oscillation).\n- **Arterial PO₂, PCO₂ and pH stay nearly normal in moderate exercise**. Above the lactate threshold, pH falls and hyperventilation lowers PCO₂ (respiratory compensation).\n- Diffusing capacity rises ≈ 3× (recruitment of pulmonary capillaries).\n- Respiration does **not** normally limit performance at sea level.\n\n## Other changes\n- Body temperature rises (up to 39–40 °C), with sweating and fluid loss.\n- Hormones: ↑catecholamines, GH, cortisol, glucagon; ↓insulin.\n- **GLUT4** translocation to muscle is insulin-independent, so exercise improves glucose uptake (useful in diabetes).\n- Haemoconcentration; leucocytosis.\n\n## Effects of training\n- **Endurance training**:\n  - ↑stroke volume (eccentric LV hypertrophy, \"athlete's heart\"), so **resting bradycardia** (40–60/min; vagal tone).\n  - ↑VO₂max (≈ 15–25%), ↑mitochondria, capillaries, myoglobin and oxidative enzymes.\n  - ↑blood volume, ↑lactate threshold, ↓BP, better lipid profile and insulin sensitivity.\n- **Resistance training**: muscle hypertrophy (type II fibres), concentric LV thickening.\n- **Detraining** reverses the gains within weeks.\n\n## Health aspects\n- **WHO 2020 recommendation**: adults need **150–300 min/week of moderate or 75–150 min/week of vigorous** aerobic activity, plus muscle strengthening on ≥ 2 days.\n- Benefits: lower risk of CHD, diabetes, hypertension, obesity, depression and some cancers. Physical inactivity is common in urban India.\n- Risks: sudden cardiac death in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, heat stroke, overtraining.\n- Exercise stress testing (treadmill, Bruce protocol) detects ischaemia.\n- Fitness tests include the Harvard step test and the 6-minute walk test.","q":{"b":["Dynamic exercise: SBP ↑, DBP same or ↓; static: both ↑.","VO₂max ≈ 35–45 mL/kg/min untrained; best fitness index.","Phosphagen ≈ 10 s, glycolysis ≈ 1–2 min, then aerobic.","Lactate threshold ≈ 50–60% VO₂max untrained.","CO 5 → 20–25 L/min; max HR ≈ 220 − age.","Stroke volume plateaus at ≈ 40–50% VO₂max.","Muscle flow ↑ up to 20×; splanchnic/renal ↓.","Ventilation up to 100–150 L/min; abrupt neural rise at onset.","PaO₂/PaCO₂ normal in moderate exercise.","EPOC = oxygen debt after exercise.","Training → resting bradycardia, ↑SV, ↑VO₂max.","Exercise translocates GLUT4 without insulin.","WHO: 150–300 min moderate per week."],"n":["Max HR 220 − age","CO up to 25 L/min","VE up to 150 L/min","VO₂max 35–45 mL/kg/min","a–vO₂ 5 → 15 mL/dL"],"m":"Energy systems **\"10 seconds, 2 minutes, then air\"** – phosphagen, glycolytic, aerobic.","v":["Best index of aerobic fitness? VO₂max.","Diastolic BP in dynamic exercise? Unchanged or slightly lower.","Cause of resting bradycardia in athletes? ↑vagal tone and ↑stroke volume."]}},"ip|High altitude":{"d":"## The problem\nBarometric pressure falls with altitude, but the **fraction of O₂ stays 20.93%**. So inspired and alveolar PO₂ fall.\n- **Sea level**: PB 760 mmHg, PIO₂ ≈ 150 mmHg, PAO₂ ≈ 100 mmHg.\n- **3000 m** (≈ 10,000 ft): PB ≈ 523, PAO₂ ≈ 60–67 mmHg, SaO₂ ≈ 90%.\n- **5500 m** (≈ 18,000 ft): PB ≈ 380 (half), PAO₂ ≈ 40 mmHg, SaO₂ ≈ 70–75%.\n- **Everest summit, 8848 m**: PB ≈ 253, PAO₂ ≈ 30–35 mmHg (climbers hyperventilate PCO₂ down to ≈ 7–10).\n\nHypoxia symptoms appear from ≈ 3000 m in unacclimatised people. Above ≈ 5500–6000 m, permanent residence is not possible. Indian relevance: Ladakh, Siachen glacier (≈ 5400–6000 m; armed forces), Himalayan pilgrimages (Amarnath, Kedarnath).\n\n## Acute responses (minutes–days)\n- **Hyperventilation**: hypoxia stimulates the **peripheral (carotid body) chemoreceptors**. This is the most important immediate response.\n  - It lowers PACO₂ and raises PAO₂.\n  - But it causes **respiratory alkalosis**, which brakes ventilation through the central chemoreceptors (the initial limitation).\n- **Tachycardia** and ↑cardiac output (the sympathetic system).\n- **Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction** causes pulmonary hypertension.\n- **Diuresis** and haemoconcentration.\n\n## Acclimatisation (days–weeks)\n- **Renal compensation**: HCO₃⁻ excretion over 2–3 days normalises CSF/blood pH. This lets ventilation rise further (**ventilatory acclimatisation**, up to ≈ 5×). Acetazolamide speeds this up.\n- **↑Erythropoietin** (HIF-1α) within hours. RBC mass rises over weeks; **haematocrit ≈ 55–60%** and Hb up to 20 g/dL.\n- **↑2,3-BPG** shifts the ODC to the right (helps unloading). This is offset by alkalosis-induced left shift at extreme altitude, where a left shift aids loading.\n- **↑Capillarity, mitochondria, myoglobin and oxidative enzymes** in tissues.\n- **Natural (genetic) adaptation** in Tibetans and Sherpas (EPAS1 variant): lower Hb, higher ventilation, more nitric oxide; versus Andeans (higher Hb).\n\n## Acute mountain sickness (AMS)\n- Appears 6–24 h after rapid ascent above ≈ 2500 m.\n- Symptoms: **headache**, nausea, fatigue, dizziness, insomnia (periodic breathing, **Cheyne–Stokes**). Lake Louise score.\n- Prevention: **gradual ascent** (above 3000 m, no more than ≈ 300–500 m/day of sleeping altitude, with a rest day every 3–4 days), \"climb high, sleep low\", **acetazolamide**, hydration.\n- Treatment: descent, O₂, acetazolamide, dexamethasone.\n\n## Life-threatening forms\n- **High-altitude pulmonary oedema (HAPE)**:\n  - Uneven hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction → overperfusion and **capillary stress failure** → high-protein oedema.\n  - Breathlessness at rest, cough, frothy sputum, crepitations, cyanosis.\n  - Treatment: **descent and O₂**, **nifedipine**, PDE-5 inhibitors (tadalafil/sildenafil), portable hyperbaric bag (Gamow).\n  - First well described in Indian soldiers; still an important cause of illness in the Indian armed forces.\n- **High-altitude cerebral oedema (HACE)**: progression of AMS with ataxia, confusion and coma. Vasogenic oedema. Treatment: **descent, O₂, dexamethasone**.\n- **Chronic mountain sickness (Monge's disease)**: in long-term residents. Excessive polycythaemia (Hb > 21 g/dL in men), pulmonary hypertension, right heart failure (cor pulmonale). Relieved by descent.\n- Other effects: retinal haemorrhages, thrombosis (polycythaemia, dehydration), frostbite, sleep disturbance, impaired judgement.\n\n## Aviation\n- Cabin pressure in aircraft is kept equivalent to ≈ 1800–2400 m (6000–8000 ft).\n- **Time of useful consciousness** after cabin decompression is ≈ 30–60 s at 10,700 m (35,000 ft) and only seconds above 15,000 m.\n- **Armstrong line ≈ 19,000 m (63,000 ft)**: PB = 47 mmHg, so body fluids boil at 37 °C and pressure suits are required.\n- 100% O₂ masks keep PAO₂ adequate up to ≈ 12,000 m.","q":{"b":["FiO₂ stays 21%; PB falls, so PO₂ falls.","PB halves at ≈ 5500 m.","Everest PB ≈ 253 mmHg.","Hyperventilation via carotid bodies is the first response.","Respiratory alkalosis initially limits ventilation.","Renal HCO₃⁻ loss allows ventilatory acclimatisation.","↑EPO → polycythaemia; ↑2,3-BPG.","AMS: headache within 6–24 h; acetazolamide.","HAPE: uneven pulmonary vasoconstriction; nifedipine, descent.","HACE: ataxia, confusion; dexamethasone, descent.","Monge's disease – chronic mountain sickness.","Sherpas/Tibetans: EPAS1 variant, lower Hb.","Armstrong line ≈ 19,000 m – body fluids boil."],"n":["PB 760 → 380 at 5500 m","Everest PB 253 mmHg","Ascent ≤ 300–500 m/day above 3000 m","Hct 55–60%"],"m":"Treatment of all altitude illness: **\"Descend, Dex, Diamox, Oxygen\"** (nifedipine for HAPE).","v":["First response to high altitude? Hyperventilation (carotid body).","Drug to prevent AMS? Acetazolamide.","Mechanism of HAPE? Uneven hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction with capillary stress failure."]}},"ip|Deep sea / hyperbaric":{"d":"## Pressure under water\nPressure rises **≈ 1 atmosphere for every 10 m (33 ft) of seawater**: 2 atm absolute at 10 m, 3 atm at 20 m, and so on.\n- **Boyle's law**: the volume of gas spaces falls (at 10 m, gas volume halves).\n- **Henry's law**: the amount of each gas dissolved in body fluids rises with its partial pressure.\n- Breath-hold diving: lung volume shrinks; the \"dive reflex\" causes bradycardia and peripheral vasoconstriction.\n\n## Barotrauma\nPressure differences across closed gas spaces.\n- **Descent (\"squeeze\")**: middle ear (most common; equalise with Valsalva), sinuses, mask.\n- **Ascent**: **pulmonary barotrauma** if the breath is held while ascending. Expanding alveolar gas ruptures alveoli, causing pneumothorax, mediastinal emphysema and **arterial gas embolism** (cerebral: stroke-like, within minutes). **Rule: never hold your breath on ascent.**\n\n## Nitrogen narcosis\n- Raised PN₂ at depth causes **\"rapture of the deep\"**: euphoria, impaired judgement and drowsiness, like alcohol intoxication.\n- Appears beyond **≈ 30–40 m**; severe beyond ≈ 60–90 m. \"**Martini law**\": each 15 m ≈ one martini.\n- Due to the lipid solubility of N₂ (anaesthetic-like effect on membranes). Reversible on ascent.\n- Prevented by substituting **helium** (heliox, trimix) for deep dives.\n- Helium at great depth (> 150–200 m) causes **high-pressure nervous syndrome** (tremor).\n\n## Oxygen toxicity\nHigh PO₂ forms **free radicals** (superoxide, hydroxyl). Hb buffering of tissue PO₂ is lost once the PO₂ is high enough to dissolve enough O₂.\n- **Acute CNS toxicity (Paul Bert effect)**: at PO₂ > **≈ 1.6–2 atm**. Twitching, nausea, visual disturbance, **seizures**. This limits pure O₂ diving to ≈ 6 m.\n- **Pulmonary toxicity (Lorrain Smith effect)**: prolonged PO₂ > ≈ 0.5 atm (e.g. > 60% O₂ for over ≈ 24 h). Tracheobronchitis, ↓surfactant, ARDS-like damage, fibrosis.\n- **Retinopathy of prematurity** in neonates on high O₂ (retrolental fibroplasia). Important in Indian NICUs; screening is recommended.\n\n## Decompression sickness (\"the bends\", caisson disease)\n- Mechanism: during a long or deep dive, **N₂ dissolves in tissues** (especially fat, which is poorly perfused but N₂ is ≈ 5× more soluble in it). **Too rapid ascent** makes N₂ come out of solution as **bubbles** in tissues and blood. The bubbles cause mechanical damage, embolism, platelet activation and inflammation.\n- **Type I (mild)**: **joint pain (\"bends\")** in knees, shoulders and elbows; skin itching and marbling; lymphatic.\n- **Type II (serious)**:\n  - neurological: **spinal cord**, paraplegia, \"staggers\"\n  - pulmonary: **\"chokes\"** (dyspnoea, cough)\n  - circulatory shock\n  - inner-ear vertigo\n- **Prevention**: slow ascent with **decompression stops** (diving tables, dive computers); no flying for ≈ 18–24 h after diving; helium mixtures (less soluble).\n- **Treatment: recompression in a hyperbaric chamber** with 100% O₂, then slow decompression; fluids.\n- Long-term: **dysbaric osteonecrosis** (head of femur/humerus).\n- Caisson workers (tunnels, bridge foundations) are also at risk.\n\n## Hyperbaric oxygen therapy (HBOT)\n100% O₂ at **2–3 atm absolute**:\n- raises dissolved O₂ (≈ 6 mL/dL at 3 atm, enough to meet resting needs without Hb)\n- shrinks bubbles\n- improves wound healing\n- displaces CO from Hb (half-life of COHb ≈ **4–5 h on room air, ≈ 60–90 min on 100% O₂, ≈ 20–30 min with HBO**)\n\nIndications:\n- **Decompression sickness**, **arterial gas embolism**\n- **Severe carbon monoxide poisoning**\n- **Gas gangrene** (Clostridium is anaerobic)\n- Diabetic foot ulcers, radiation necrosis, crush injury, compromised grafts\n\nComplications: barotrauma, O₂ toxicity, claustrophobia.\n\n## SCUBA\nSelf-Contained Underwater Breathing Apparatus. A **demand regulator** delivers air at the ambient pressure (Cousteau–Gagnan). Recreational diving limit ≈ 30–40 m. Saturation diving with helium allows prolonged deep work.\n\n## Submarine escape\nFree ascent with continuous exhalation, or with escape suits. CO₂ build-up is a hazard in submarines.","q":{"b":["+1 atm per 10 m of seawater.","Boyle's law – gas spaces shrink on descent.","Never hold breath on ascent – arterial gas embolism.","Nitrogen narcosis beyond 30–40 m; 'Martini law'.","Helium replaces nitrogen for deep dives.","CNS O₂ toxicity at PO₂ > 1.6–2 atm – seizures (Paul Bert).","Pulmonary O₂ toxicity – prolonged high FiO₂ (Lorrain Smith).","Decompression sickness – N₂ bubbles on rapid ascent.","Type I bends: joint pain; type II: CNS, chokes.","Treatment – hyperbaric recompression.","HBOT 2–3 atm: CO poisoning, gas gangrene, DCS.","Dysbaric osteonecrosis – late effect.","No flying for 18–24 h after diving."],"n":["1 atm/10 m","Narcosis > 30 m","O₂ seizures > 1.6 atm","HBOT 2–3 ATA","COHb t½ 4–5 h → 20–30 min with HBO"],"m":"Decompression sickness: **\"Bends, Chokes, Staggers\"** – joints, lungs, CNS.","v":["Gas causing decompression sickness? Nitrogen.","Treatment of decompression sickness? Recompression in a hyperbaric chamber.","Why is helium used in deep diving? Less narcotic and less soluble."]}},"ip|Acid–base disorders":{"d":"## Normal values (arterial)\n- **pH 7.35–7.45** (H⁺ ≈ 40 nmol/L)\n- **PaCO₂ 35–45 mmHg**\n- **HCO₃⁻ 22–26 mmol/L**\n- Base excess −2 to +2\n- Anion gap **≈ 8–16 mmol/L** (Na⁺ − [Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻]); ≈ 10–12 typical, lower with newer analysers\n\n**Henderson–Hasselbalch**: pH = 6.1 + log [HCO₃⁻ / (0.03 × PCO₂)]. A **20:1 ratio** gives pH 7.4. The kidney controls the numerator and the lungs the denominator.\n\nDefence lines:\n1. **Buffers** (seconds): bicarbonate, haemoglobin, proteins, phosphate\n2. **Respiratory** (minutes–hours)\n3. **Renal** (hours–days): H⁺ secretion, HCO₃⁻ reabsorption and generation, NH₄⁺ excretion\n\n## Stepwise interpretation of an ABG\n1. Acidaemia or alkalaemia (pH)?\n2. Primary disturbance: does PCO₂ or HCO₃⁻ explain the pH?\n3. Is compensation appropriate?\n4. In metabolic acidosis, calculate the **anion gap**.\n5. Is there a mixed disorder (delta ratio)?\n6. Correlate with clinical findings.\n\n## Metabolic acidosis (primary ↓HCO₃⁻)\n- **Compensation**: hyperventilation (**Kussmaul breathing**). **Winter's formula**: expected PCO₂ = 1.5 × HCO₃⁻ + 8 ± 2.\n- **High anion gap** (unmeasured acids added). Mnemonic **MUDPILES**: Methanol, Uraemia, **Diabetic ketoacidosis**, Paraldehyde/Paracetamol, Iron/Isoniazid, **Lactic acidosis** (shock, sepsis, metformin), Ethylene glycol, Salicylates. Methanol poisoning (hooch tragedies) is relevant in India.\n- **Normal anion gap (hyperchloraemic)**: HCO₃⁻ loss (**diarrhoea**, a common cause in Indian children; ureterosigmoidostomy), **renal tubular acidosis**, acetazolamide, saline infusion. Urinary anion gap helps separate gut from renal loss.\n- Effects: ↓myocardial contractility, arrhythmias, vasodilatation, **hyperkalaemia** (H⁺/K⁺ shift), ↓response to catecholamines, bone demineralisation (chronic).\n\n## Metabolic alkalosis (primary ↑HCO₃⁻)\n- **Compensation**: hypoventilation (limited by hypoxia); PCO₂ rises ≈ 0.7 per 1 mmol/L rise of HCO₃⁻.\n- Causes:\n  - **Vomiting/nasogastric suction** (loss of HCl; pyloric stenosis), **diuretics** (loops, thiazides)\n  - Mineralocorticoid excess (Conn's, Cushing's)\n  - Bartter/Gitelman syndromes\n  - Alkali ingestion (milk-alkali)\n  - Hypokalaemia\n- **Maintenance** needs volume depletion, Cl⁻ depletion and hypokalaemia. Hence **chloride-responsive** (urine Cl⁻ < 20; responds to saline) vs **chloride-resistant** (urine Cl⁻ > 20; mineralocorticoid excess).\n- Paradoxical aciduria in prolonged vomiting.\n- Effects: **↓ionised Ca²⁺ → tetany**, hypokalaemia, arrhythmias, hypoventilation, left shift of the ODC.\n\n## Respiratory acidosis (primary ↑PCO₂)\nHypoventilation.\n- Causes: **COPD**, severe asthma, CNS depression (opioids, sedatives), neuromuscular weakness (GBS, myasthenia, organophosphate poisoning: very common in rural India), chest-wall disease, obesity hypoventilation, airway obstruction.\n- **Compensation**: renal HCO₃⁻ retention.\n  - **Acute**: HCO₃⁻ ↑ 1 per 10 mmHg rise in PCO₂.\n  - **Chronic**: ↑ ≈ 3.5–4 per 10.\n- Effects: CO₂ narcosis, headache, cerebral vasodilatation (↑ICP), flapping tremor.\n\n## Respiratory alkalosis (primary ↓PCO₂)\nHyperventilation.\n- Causes: **anxiety/hyperventilation syndrome**, **high altitude**, hypoxaemia, pulmonary embolism, pneumonia, early salicylate poisoning, sepsis, liver failure, pregnancy (progesterone), over-ventilation on a ventilator.\n- **Compensation**: renal HCO₃⁻ excretion.\n  - **Acute**: HCO₃⁻ ↓ 2 per 10 mmHg fall.\n  - **Chronic**: ↓ 4–5 per 10.\n- Effects: **↓ionised Ca²⁺**, so **tetany** with carpopedal spasm and perioral tingling, lightheadedness (cerebral vasoconstriction), syncope.\n\n## Mixed disorders\nCompensation never overcorrects pH; if it seems to, think of a mixed disorder. Examples:\n- **Salicylate poisoning**: respiratory alkalosis + high-AG metabolic acidosis.\n- Cardiac arrest: respiratory + metabolic acidosis.\n- Vomiting in diabetic ketoacidosis.\n- The delta ratio (ΔAG/ΔHCO₃⁻) identifies coexisting metabolic disorders.\n\n## Treatment principles\nTreat the cause.\n- Bicarbonate is only for severe acidosis (pH < 7.1–7.2) or HCO₃⁻ loss.\n- Saline and KCl for chloride-responsive alkalosis.\n- Ventilatory support for respiratory acidosis.\n- Rebreathing or reassurance for anxiety hyperventilation.","q":{"b":["pH 7.35–7.45; PaCO₂ 35–45; HCO₃⁻ 22–26.","HCO₃⁻:H₂CO₃ = 20:1 at pH 7.4.","Anion gap = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻) ≈ 8–16.","High-AG acidosis: MUDPILES.","Normal-AG acidosis: diarrhoea, RTA.","Winter's: PCO₂ = 1.5 × HCO₃⁻ + 8 ± 2.","Kussmaul breathing in metabolic acidosis.","Vomiting → hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis.","Chloride-responsive alkalosis – saline.","Respiratory acidosis: COPD, opioids, OP poisoning.","Chronic respiratory acidosis: HCO₃⁻ ↑ 3.5–4 per 10 mmHg.","Alkalosis → ↓ionised Ca²⁺ → tetany.","Salicylates: mixed respiratory alkalosis + metabolic acidosis.","Compensation never overcorrects pH."],"n":["pH 7.35–7.45","PaCO₂ 35–45 mmHg","HCO₃⁻ 22–26","AG 8–16","20:1 ratio"],"m":"High-AG acidosis **\"MUDPILES\"** – Methanol, Uraemia, DKA, Paracetamol, INH/Iron, Lactate, Ethylene glycol, Salicylates.","v":["Breathing pattern in DKA? Kussmaul breathing.","Acid–base disorder in pyloric stenosis? Hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis.","Normal anion gap acidosis in children? Diarrhoea."]}},"ip|Nutrition & BMR":{"d":"## Energy units and values\n- 1 kcal = 4.184 kJ.\n- Physiological fuel values (Atwater): **carbohydrate 4 kcal/g, protein 4 kcal/g, fat 9 kcal/g, alcohol 7 kcal/g**.\n- In a bomb calorimeter protein gives ≈ 5.3 kcal/g, but urea is excreted unburnt.\n- **Respiratory quotient (RQ = CO₂ produced/O₂ consumed)**: carbohydrate **1.0**, fat **0.7**, protein **0.8**, mixed diet **≈ 0.82–0.85**. RQ > 1 occurs in lipogenesis (fattening) and hyperventilation; < 0.7 in ketosis/starvation.\n- **Calorific value of O₂ ≈ 4.825 kcal/L** for a mixed diet (≈ 5.0 for carbohydrate, 4.7 for fat).\n\n## Basal metabolic rate\nThe energy needed to maintain vital functions at rest. Standard conditions:\n- **12–14 h after the last meal** (post-absorptive)\n- **physical and mental rest**, lying awake, after a good night's sleep\n- **thermoneutral** environment (≈ 20–25 °C)\n- normal body temperature, no emotional stress\n\n**Normal BMR**:\n- ≈ **40 kcal/m²/h in adult men** (≈ 35–40), ≈ 5–10% lower in women (≈ 37).\n- In total ≈ **1500–1800 kcal/day for men**, 1200–1400 for women.\n- Roughly **1 kcal/kg/h**.\n- Basal metabolism accounts for ≈ 60–70% of total daily energy expenditure in sedentary people.\n\n### Measurement\n- **Direct calorimetry** (Atwater–Benedict chamber) measures heat directly.\n- **Indirect calorimetry** measures **O₂ consumption**, e.g. the **Benedict–Roth spirometer** (closed circuit, CO₂ absorbed by soda lime; O₂ uptake from the slope of the trace), the Douglas bag, or metabolic carts.\n- **BMR = O₂ consumption (L/h, corrected to STPD) × 4.825 ÷ body surface area**.\n- Surface area from the **DuBois formula** (height, weight) or a nomogram.\n- Results are expressed as % deviation from standard tables (normal **±15%**).\n- Prediction equations: Harris–Benedict, FAO/WHO/UNU (Schofield); ICMR uses the latter for Indians.\n\n### Factors affecting BMR\n- **↑**: **hyperthyroidism (up to +50 to +100%)**, fever (≈ **+10–13% per °C**, Q₁₀ effect), growth/childhood, pregnancy (2nd/3rd trimester), lactation, catecholamines, stress, cold climate (long term), muscular build, recent food (SDA), smoking, caffeine, Cushing's syndrome, burns, sepsis, cancer.\n- **↓**: **hypothyroidism (down to −30 to −40%)**, starvation/malnutrition (adaptive), sleep (≈ 10–15% lower), old age (≈ 2% per decade after 20), female sex, Addison's disease, hypopituitarism, hypothermia.\n- BMR correlates best with **lean body mass** (surface area is the classical basis).\n- BMR was historically used as a thyroid function test; now replaced by TSH/T₄.\n\n## Thermic effect of food (SDA)\nThe obligatory rise in heat production after a meal (digestion, absorption, synthesis):\n- **protein ≈ 20–30%** of its energy value (deamination, urea and protein synthesis)\n- carbohydrate ≈ 5–10%\n- fat ≈ 0–3%\n- a mixed diet ≈ **10%** of total daily energy\n\n## Total energy expenditure\nTEE = BMR + thermic effect of food (≈ 10%) + physical activity (most variable).\n- Expressed as the **Physical Activity Level (PAL = TEE/BMR)**: sedentary ≈ 1.4–1.5, moderate ≈ 1.7–1.8, heavy ≈ 2.0–2.4.\n- **ICMR-NIN 2020 RDA (reference Indian man 65 kg, woman 55 kg)**: sedentary man ≈ **2110 kcal/day**, moderate ≈ 2710, heavy ≈ 3470; woman ≈ 1660 / 2130 / 2720 kcal/day. Pregnancy adds ≈ **+350 kcal/day** (2nd/3rd trimester); lactation ≈ +600 (0–6 months).\n\n## Balanced diet\n- **Proportions**: carbohydrate ≈ 50–60% of energy, fat ≈ 20–30%, protein ≈ 10–15%.\n- **Protein RDA ≈ 0.83 g/kg/day** (ICMR 2020; ≈ 54 g for the reference man, 46 g for the woman); more in pregnancy, lactation and childhood.\n- **Biological value**: egg ≈ 94–100 (reference protein). Animal proteins have higher BV. Pulse–cereal combination (dal–rice/roti) complements lysine (pulses) and methionine (cereals).\n- **Essential amino acids** (adults 8 + histidine in infants; \"PVT TIM HALL\").\n- **Essential fatty acids**: linoleic (ω-6), α-linolenic (ω-3).\n- **Fibre** ≈ 25–40 g/day.\n- **Nitrogen balance**: positive in growth, pregnancy and convalescence; negative in starvation, fever, burns, trauma. 6.25 g protein = 1 g N.\n\n## Malnutrition (India)\nNFHS-5 (2019–21) showed under-5 stunting ≈ 35%, wasting ≈ 19% and underweight ≈ 32%, alongside rising adult obesity: the **double burden**.\n- **Kwashiorkor**: protein deficiency with relatively adequate calories. Oedema (low albumin), fatty liver, skin and hair changes, apathy.\n- **Marasmus**: deficiency of total calories. Severe wasting, \"old-man face\", no oedema.\n- Programmes: POSHAN Abhiyaan, mid-day meals, ICDS.","q":{"b":["Carbohydrate 4, protein 4, fat 9, alcohol 7 kcal/g.","RQ: carbohydrate 1.0, fat 0.7, protein 0.8, mixed ≈ 0.82.","Calorific value of O₂ ≈ 4.825 kcal/L.","BMR ≈ 40 kcal/m²/h (men); normal range ±15%.","BMR conditions: 12–14 h fast, rest, thermoneutral.","Benedict–Roth spirometer – indirect calorimetry.","BMR ↑ hyperthyroidism, fever (+13%/°C), pregnancy.","BMR ↓ hypothyroidism, starvation, sleep, age.","SDA highest for protein (≈ 30%).","ICMR 2020: sedentary man ≈ 2110 kcal/day.","Protein RDA ≈ 0.83 g/kg/day.","Pregnancy +350 kcal; lactation +600 kcal.","Kwashiorkor – oedema; marasmus – wasting."],"n":["BMR 40 kcal/m²/h","RQ mixed 0.82","O₂ 4.825 kcal/L","Protein 0.83 g/kg/day","SDA protein 30%"],"m":"Essential amino acids **\"PVT TIM HALL\"** – Phe, Val, Thr, Trp, Ile, Met, His, Arg, Leu, Lys.","v":["RQ of fat? 0.7.","Food with the highest SDA? Protein.","Instrument for measuring BMR in the lab? Benedict–Roth spirometer."]}},"ip|Ageing & yoga":{"d":"## Ageing\nAgeing is a progressive, time-dependent decline in physiological reserve and adaptability, which raises vulnerability to disease and death. Indian life expectancy is ≈ 70 years. The population over 60 is ≈ 10% and rising.\n\n### Theories\n- **Programmed theories**: **telomere shortening** (Hayflick limit ≈ 50 divisions in cultured fibroblasts), neuroendocrine and immune (immunosenescence) clocks.\n- **Damage (stochastic) theories**: **free radical/oxidative stress** (Harman), mitochondrial DNA damage, protein cross-linking/glycation (AGEs), DNA mutation, cellular senescence (SASP inflammation, \"inflammageing\").\n- Caloric restriction extends lifespan in animals (sirtuins, mTOR, insulin/IGF-1 signalling).\n\n### Changes by system\n- **CVS**: arterial stiffening, so **↑systolic BP and pulse pressure** (isolated systolic hypertension); ↓maximum heart rate (220 − age); ↓β-adrenergic responsiveness; ↓baroreflex sensitivity (postural hypotension, falls); LV hypertrophy, diastolic dysfunction.\n- **Respiratory**: ↓elastic recoil, ↑compliance of lung but ↓chest-wall compliance; **↓FEV₁ (≈ 25–30 mL/yr)** and vital capacity; **↑residual volume** and closing volume; ↓PaO₂ (≈ 100 − age/3 mmHg); weaker cough.\n- **Renal**: **GFR falls ≈ 1 mL/min/yr after 40** although creatinine stays similar (↓muscle mass), so drug doses must be adjusted; ↓concentrating ability and thirst, so prone to dehydration and hyponatraemia.\n- **Nervous system**: ↓neurons and brain weight; ↓processing speed, working memory and reaction time; ↓deep sleep; benign forgetfulness vs dementia.\n- **Special senses**: presbyopia, cataract, **presbycusis** (high frequencies), ↓taste and smell.\n- **Musculoskeletal**: **sarcopenia** (loss of muscle mass and strength ≈ 1–2%/yr after 50, type II fibres) and **osteoporosis** (women after menopause: loss of oestrogen).\n- **Endocrine**: menopause (≈ 46–48 years in Indian women), andropause (gradual ↓testosterone), ↓GH (somatopause), ↓DHEA, insulin resistance, ↓vitamin D synthesis.\n- **GI**: ↓acid, constipation, ↓drug metabolism.\n- **Immune**: thymic involution, ↓T-cell responses, more infections and cancer, weaker vaccine responses.\n- **Thermoregulation**: ↓shivering and sweating, prone to hypothermia and heat stroke.\n- **Body composition**: ↑fat, ↓lean mass and ↓total body water.\n\n### Healthy ageing\nPhysical activity, especially resistance and balance training (prevents falls), adequate protein, calcium and vitamin D, cognitive and social engagement, no smoking, vaccination. National Programme for Health Care of the Elderly (NPHCE).\n\n## Yoga\nYoga is an ancient Indian system. **Patanjali's ashtanga** has eight limbs: yama, niyama, **asana** (postures), **pranayama** (breath control), pratyahara, **dharana, dhyana** (meditation), samadhi. **International Day of Yoga is 21 June**. Yoga is included in the **CBME foundation course** (yoga orientation for MBBS students).\n\n### Physiological effects (evidence varies in quality)\n- **Autonomic**: shift towards **parasympathetic dominance**: ↓resting heart rate, **↑heart-rate variability**, ↓BP (modest ≈ 5–10 mmHg in hypertensives), ↓catecholamines.\n- **Respiratory**: slow breathing (≈ 6/min, as in pranayama) improves baroreflex sensitivity and respiratory sinus arrhythmia; may improve PEFR, breath-holding time and respiratory muscle strength. Yogis can reduce breathing rate and O₂ consumption.\n- **Metabolic and endocrine**: ↓cortisol (stress), improved insulin sensitivity and glycaemic control, ↓weight and lipids (adjunct in type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome).\n- **Musculoskeletal**: flexibility, balance, strength; helpful in low back pain.\n- **Neuropsychological**: ↓anxiety, stress and depression; better attention and sleep; EEG shows **↑alpha waves** during meditation (↑theta in deep meditation); ↑GABA reported.\n- **Specific practices**:\n  - **Anulom-vilom** (alternate-nostril breathing) and **bhramari** (humming: ↑nasal NO).\n  - **Kapalbhati** (rapid forceful exhalation).\n  - **Shavasana** (relaxation; reduces BP).\n\n### Cautions\nYoga is a complement to, not a replacement for, medical treatment. Some postures are risky in glaucoma (inversions), uncontrolled hypertension and spinal disease. Many studies are small; the strongest evidence is for stress, back pain and modest BP and glycaemic benefits.","q":{"b":["Telomere shortening – Hayflick limit ≈ 50 divisions.","Free radical theory of ageing (Harman).","Ageing ↑SBP and pulse pressure (stiff arteries).","Maximum heart rate ≈ 220 − age.","GFR ↓ ≈ 1 mL/min/yr after 40; creatinine may stay normal.","↑Residual volume, ↓FEV₁ and VC with age.","Sarcopenia and osteoporosis → falls and fractures.","Presbyopia and presbycusis.","Menopause ≈ 46–48 years in Indian women.","Ashtanga yoga has 8 limbs (Patanjali).","Yoga → parasympathetic dominance, ↑HRV.","Meditation ↑alpha waves on EEG.","International Day of Yoga – 21 June."],"n":["Hayflick ≈ 50 divisions","GFR ↓ 1 mL/min/yr","FEV₁ ↓ 25–30 mL/yr","Pranayama ≈ 6 breaths/min"],"m":"Ageing **\"The 4 Is\"** – Immobility, Instability (falls), Incontinence, Intellectual impairment.","v":["Why is serum creatinine normal in the elderly despite ↓GFR? Reduced muscle mass.","Main autonomic effect of yoga? Parasympathetic dominance.","EEG wave increased in meditation? Alpha."]}},"ip|Obesity & metabolic syndrome":{"d":"## Definitions\n**BMI = weight (kg)/height² (m²).**\n- **WHO**: normal 18.5–24.9, overweight 25–29.9, obese ≥ 30.\n- **Asian-Indian cut-offs** (consensus 2009, used in India): normal 18.0–22.9, **overweight 23–24.9, obese ≥ 25**. These are lower because Indians have more body fat and visceral fat at a given BMI (the **\"thin-fat Indian\" phenotype**).\n- **Waist circumference** (central/abdominal obesity) for Indians: **≥ 90 cm men, ≥ 80 cm women**. Waist–hip ratio > 0.9 (men) / > 0.85 (women). Waist-to-height ratio > 0.5.\n- BMI does not distinguish fat from muscle; body fat can be measured by skinfold calipers, bioimpedance, DEXA or underwater weighing.\n\n## Regulation of energy balance\n**Arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus**:\n- **Orexigenic (↑intake)** neurons: **NPY/AgRP**.\n- **Anorexigenic (↓intake)** neurons: **POMC → α-MSH**, acting on **MC4R** (paraventricular nucleus); CART.\n- Lateral hypothalamus (orexin, MCH) was the classical \"feeding centre\"; ventromedial nucleus the \"satiety centre\". **VMH lesions** cause hyperphagia and obesity; **LH lesions** cause aphagia.\n\nPeripheral signals:\n- **Leptin** (adipocytes; long-term signal proportional to fat mass): inhibits NPY/AgRP and stimulates POMC.\n- **Insulin**: long-term satiety.\n- **Ghrelin** (stomach fundus): the **only orexigenic gut hormone**; rises before meals.\n- **CCK, GLP-1, PYY₃₋₃₆**, oxyntomodulin, gastric distension (vagal): short-term satiety.\n\nCommon human obesity involves **leptin resistance** (high leptin), not deficiency. Rare monogenic causes: leptin or leptin-receptor deficiency (responds to leptin), **MC4R mutations** (commonest monogenic), POMC deficiency.\n\n## Causes of obesity\n- **Positive energy balance**: energy-dense diet, sugar-sweetened drinks, sedentary lifestyle, screen time, sleep deprivation.\n- Genetics (heritability ≈ 40–70%; FTO gene).\n- **Thrifty phenotype hypothesis** (Barker; DOHaD): low birth weight followed by rapid catch-up growth. Very relevant to India.\n- Endocrine (minority): hypothyroidism, Cushing's, PCOS, GH deficiency, hypothalamic damage.\n- Drugs: steroids, antipsychotics, insulin.\n- Syndromes: Prader–Willi.\n- Gut microbiota (emerging).\n\n## Adipose tissue as an endocrine organ\n- **Leptin**; **adiponectin** (insulin-sensitising, anti-inflammatory; *reduced* in obesity).\n- Resistin, TNF-α, IL-6, PAI-1, angiotensinogen, aromatase (oestrogen).\n- **Visceral fat** is more harmful than subcutaneous: free fatty acids drain into the portal vein (hepatic insulin resistance, NAFLD), plus inflammatory cytokines.\n\n## Metabolic syndrome\nA cluster of risk factors centred on **insulin resistance**/central obesity. Doubles CVD risk and raises type 2 diabetes risk ≈ 5×.\n\n**Harmonised (IDF/AHA 2009) criteria: any 3 of 5**:\n1. **Waist** ≥ 90 cm (men) / ≥ 80 cm (women): South Asian cut-offs\n2. **Triglycerides ≥ 150 mg/dL** (or on treatment)\n3. **HDL < 40 mg/dL** (men) / **< 50 mg/dL** (women)\n4. **BP ≥ 130/85 mmHg** (or on treatment)\n5. **Fasting glucose ≥ 100 mg/dL** (or on treatment)\n\nUnder IDF 2005, central obesity was mandatory. Prevalence in urban Indian adults is ≈ 30% or more.\n\nAssociated conditions: NAFLD/MASLD, PCOS, obstructive sleep apnoea, hyperuricaemia/gout, prothrombotic state, CKD.\n\n## Physiological consequences of obesity\n- **CVS**: ↑blood volume and cardiac output, hypertension, LV hypertrophy, atherosclerosis, heart failure, atrial fibrillation.\n- **Respiratory**: ↓chest-wall compliance, **↓FRC and ERV**, V/Q mismatch, **obesity hypoventilation (Pickwickian) syndrome**, sleep apnoea.\n- **Metabolic**: type 2 diabetes, dyslipidaemia, gallstones.\n- Others: osteoarthritis, certain cancers (endometrial, breast, colon), infertility, depression.\n\n## Management\n- **Lifestyle**: calorie deficit ≈ 500 kcal/day (≈ 0.5 kg/week loss), activity ≥ 150–300 min/week, behavioural therapy. **5–10% weight loss improves risk factors** substantially.\n- **Drugs**: orlistat (lipase inhibitor); **GLP-1 receptor agonists** (semaglutide, liraglutide) and tirzepatide (GIP/GLP-1).\n- **Bariatric surgery**: sleeve gastrectomy, Roux-en-Y bypass. Indicated for BMI ≥ 35, or ≥ 30–32.5 with comorbidity in Asians. Improves or reverses diabetes (incretin changes).","q":{"b":["BMI = kg/m².","Asian-Indian: overweight 23–24.9; obese ≥ 25.","Indian waist cut-off: ≥ 90 cm men, ≥ 80 cm women.","Thin-fat Indian: more visceral fat at a lower BMI.","NPY/AgRP ↑ intake; POMC/α-MSH → MC4R ↓ intake.","VMH lesion → obesity; LH lesion → aphagia.","Leptin from fat – long-term satiety; obesity = leptin resistance.","Ghrelin – the only orexigenic gut hormone.","MC4R mutation – commonest monogenic obesity.","Adiponectin ↓ in obesity.","Metabolic syndrome: 3 of 5 (waist, TG, HDL, BP, glucose).","Obesity ↓FRC and ERV.","5–10% weight loss improves risk."],"n":["BMI ≥ 25 obese (Asian)","Waist 90/80 cm","TG ≥ 150","HDL < 40/50","BP ≥ 130/85","FBG ≥ 100"],"m":"Metabolic syndrome **\"WHAT-B\"**: Waist, HDL, (Arterial) pressure, Triglycerides, Blood sugar.","v":["Hormone that stimulates appetite? Ghrelin.","Obesity cut-off BMI for Indians? 25 kg/m².","Main problem in common obesity? Leptin resistance."]}},"ip|Infancy & growth charts":{"d":"## Fetal to neonatal transition\n- **First breath**: compression of the chest in the birth canal, cooling, tactile stimuli, hypoxia/hypercapnia, and clamping of the cord. A large negative pressure (≈ −40 to −60 cmH₂O, higher in some estimates) is needed to open the fluid-filled alveoli. **Surfactant** (made from ≈ 24 weeks, adequate by ≈ 34–35 weeks) prevents collapse. Lung fluid is absorbed by ENaC (catecholamine-driven).\n- **Circulation** changes from fetal to adult:\n  - lung expansion → ↓pulmonary resistance; cord clamping → ↑systemic resistance\n  - left atrial pressure rises → **foramen ovale closes** (functionally at once, anatomically in months)\n  - **ductus arteriosus constricts** (↑PO₂, ↓PGE₂) within ≈ 1–3 days, anatomically by 2–3 weeks (ligamentum arteriosum); patent in prematurity (indomethacin/ibuprofen/paracetamol to close)\n  - ductus venosus closes\n- **Neonatal values**: heart rate **120–160/min**, respiratory rate **40–60/min**, BP ≈ 70/45 mmHg, Hb ≈ 17–19 g/dL (HbF ≈ 70–80%).\n\n## Problems of neonatal physiology\n- **Thermoregulation**: large surface area/mass, thin fat, no shivering, so **brown fat** (non-shivering thermogenesis) does the work. Prone to hypothermia. **Kangaroo mother care** for low-birth-weight babies.\n- **Physiological jaundice**: day 2–3, peaks day 3–5, clears by 1–2 weeks (3 in preterm). Causes: shorter RBC life (≈ 70–90 days), ↑RBC mass, **immature UDP-glucuronyl transferase**, ↑enterohepatic circulation. Jaundice in the first 24 h is pathological. Kernicterus risk; phototherapy.\n- **Physiological weight loss**: up to 7–10% in the first week; birth weight regained by **≈ 10–14 days**.\n- Renal: ↓GFR and concentrating ability.\n- Immunity: maternal IgG (transplacental), IgA (colostrum).\n- Vitamin K deficiency: give **vitamin K at birth** to prevent haemorrhagic disease.\n- **Physiological anaemia of infancy**: nadir at ≈ 8–12 weeks (Hb ≈ 9.5–11 g/dL). EPO falls after birth because of the higher PO₂.\n\n## Breastfeeding\n**Exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months**, then complementary food with breastfeeding continued to 2 years (WHO/India). Start within 1 hour of birth. Colostrum is rich in IgA, protein and vitamin A.\n\n## Growth\n### Weight\n- **Birth weight in India ≈ 2.8–3.0 kg**. **LBW < 2500 g** (≈ 18–20% of Indian births).\n- Weight **doubles by ≈ 5 months, triples by 1 year**, ×4 by 2 years.\n- **Weech's formula** (approx.): 3–12 months: (age in months + 9)/2 kg; 1–6 yr: age × 2 + 8 kg.\n\n### Length/height\n- ≈ **50 cm at birth**, **75 cm at 1 year**, ≈ 87 cm at 2 years, **≈ 100 cm at 4 years** (double birth length).\n- Growth velocity is highest in infancy (≈ 25 cm in year 1), then ≈ 5–6 cm/yr, then the **pubertal spurt** (girls ≈ 2 years before boys).\n\n### Head circumference\n- ≈ **34–35 cm at birth**, ≈ 45–47 cm at 1 year.\n- Anterior fontanelle closes by **≈ 12–18 months**; posterior by ≈ 2–3 months.\n\n### Others\nTeeth: first erupt ≈ 6 months (lower central incisor); all 20 deciduous teeth by ≈ 2½ years.\n\n## Growth charts\n- **WHO Child Growth Standards (2006)** (0–5 years) describe how children *should* grow under optimal conditions (breastfed, healthy, non-smoking mothers; multicentre including India). **Adopted by India** in the Mother and Child Protection card.\n- **IAP 2015 growth charts** for 5–18 years (Indian data).\n- Plots used: weight-for-age, length/height-for-age, weight-for-height, BMI-for-age, head circumference. Read as **percentiles** (3rd–97th) or **Z-scores**.\n- **Stunting**: height-for-age < −2 SD (chronic undernutrition).\n- **Wasting**: weight-for-height < −2 SD (acute). **Severe acute malnutrition**: < −3 SD, or MUAC < 11.5 cm, or bilateral oedema.\n- **Underweight**: weight-for-age < −2 SD.\n- **Growth monitoring** is serial plotting; the direction of the curve (faltering, crossing percentiles) matters more than a single point. Used in **ICDS/Anganwadi** centres.\n\n## Hormones of growth\n- **Infancy**: mainly nutrition and insulin.\n- **Childhood**: **GH–IGF-1** and thyroid hormone.\n- **Puberty**: sex steroids plus GH (spurt; epiphyseal fusion by oestrogen).\n\nBone age (X-ray of the wrist) assesses maturity.\n\n## Development milestones (brief)\n- Social smile 6 weeks\n- Head control 3 months\n- Sits without support 6–8 months\n- Stands 9–10 months\n- Walks ≈ 12–13 months\n- Words ≈ 1 year\n- Two-word phrases ≈ 2 years","q":{"b":["Ductus arteriosus closes by ↑PO₂ and ↓PGE₂.","Foramen ovale closes functionally when LA pressure rises.","Neonatal HR 120–160, RR 40–60.","Brown fat – non-shivering thermogenesis.","Physiological jaundice – day 2–3; never in the first 24 h.","Birth weight regained by 10–14 days.","Weight ×2 at 5 months, ×3 at 1 year.","Length 50 cm birth, 75 cm at 1 year, 100 cm at 4 years.","Head circumference 34–35 cm at birth.","LBW < 2500 g.","WHO 2006 standards (0–5 yr) adopted in India.","Stunting – height-for-age < −2 SD; wasting – weight-for-height.","Exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months."],"n":["HR 120–160","RR 40–60","LBW < 2.5 kg","HC 35 cm","Length 50 → 75 cm at 1 yr"],"m":"Weight **\"Double at 5, Triple at 12\"** (months).","v":["Index of chronic undernutrition? Stunting (height-for-age).","When does birth weight triple? About 1 year.","Fuel for neonatal heat production? Brown fat."]}},"ip|Brain death":{"d":"## Concept\n**Brain death** is the irreversible loss of all brain function, **including the brainstem**. In India it is legally recognised as death (\"**brainstem death**\") under the **Transplantation of Human Organs and Tissues Act (THOTA) 1994**, amended 2011, with Rules of 2014.\n- The heart may keep beating with ventilator support, but the person is dead.\n- Brain death declaration is the basis of **deceased (cadaveric) organ donation**.\n- It must be distinguished from:\n  - **coma**: unresponsive, but the brainstem may function\n  - **persistent vegetative state**: sleep–wake cycles and brainstem reflexes intact, cortex lost; patients may breathe spontaneously\n  - **locked-in syndrome**: ventral pontine lesion; conscious, communicates with vertical eye movements and blinking\n\n## Preconditions (before testing)\n1. A known, irreversible cause of brain damage (head injury, intracranial haemorrhage) consistent with brain death.\n2. **Exclude reversible causes**:\n   - sedatives, opioids, neuromuscular blockers (check drug levels and the time elapsed)\n   - **hypothermia** (core < 34–35 °C)\n   - severe metabolic/endocrine disturbance (glucose, Na⁺, uraemia, hepatic encephalopathy)\n   - **shock** (hypotension)\n3. The patient is in deep coma, apnoeic and on a ventilator.\n\n## Clinical tests (absent brainstem reflexes)\n- **Pupils**: fixed, **no response to light** (CN II, III; midbrain); usually mid-size or dilated.\n- **Corneal reflex** absent (V afferent, VII efferent; pons).\n- **Oculocephalic (doll's-eye) reflex** absent: eyes move with the head (VIII, MLF, III/VI).\n- **Vestibulo-ocular (caloric) reflex** absent: no eye movement with 50 mL ice-cold water in each ear (canals clear) over ≈ 1 min of observation.\n- **No motor response within the cranial-nerve distribution** to painful stimuli (supraorbital pressure). Spinal reflexes (withdrawal, deep tendon, Lazarus sign) **may persist** and do not exclude brain death.\n- **Gag and cough reflexes** absent (IX, X; medulla); tested by suction catheter down the endotracheal tube.\n- **Apnoea test** (medulla respiratory centre):\n  1. preoxygenate with 100% O₂ for 10 min\n  2. ensure PaCO₂ ≈ 40 mmHg\n  3. disconnect from the ventilator with O₂ insufflation via the tracheal catheter\n  4. observe for **no respiratory effort** as **PaCO₂ rises to ≥ 60 mmHg** (or ≥ 20 mmHg above baseline)\n  5. stop if the patient becomes unstable (hypotension, arrhythmia, desaturation)\n\n## Indian procedure (THOTA)\n- Certification by a **Board of Medical Experts** of **4 doctors**:\n  1. the registered medical practitioner in charge of the hospital\n  2. an independent specialist nominated from an approved panel\n  3. a neurologist/neurosurgeon (or, if unavailable, an anaesthetist/intensivist, per the 2014 rules) nominated from the panel\n  4. the treating doctor\n- Members of the transplant team must not be on the board.\n- **Tests are done twice, ≈ 6 hours apart**. Time of death is recorded at the second test.\n- Form 10 is used for certification.\n- **NOTTO** (National Organ and Tissue Transplant Organisation) coordinates allocation.\n- Ancillary tests are not mandatory in India but are used when clinical testing is incomplete (facial trauma, high cervical injury): **EEG (electrocerebral silence)**, cerebral angiography/four-vessel or radionuclide scan showing **no intracranial blood flow**, transcranial Doppler.\n\n## Physiological changes after brain death\n- **Cushing's response / sympathetic storm** during herniation (hypertension, tachycardia, arrhythmias), then **loss of sympathetic tone → hypotension**.\n- **Diabetes insipidus** (loss of posterior pituitary ADH): polyuria, hypernatraemia.\n- **Poikilothermia** (hypothalamic failure): hypothermia.\n- Hypothyroidism, hyperglycaemia, coagulopathy (tissue factor release), neurogenic pulmonary oedema.\n- **Donor management** keeps organs viable: fluids, vasopressors, **vasopressin/desmopressin**, warming, ventilation, hormone therapy (T₃/T₄, steroids).\n- Without support, cardiac arrest usually follows within days.\n\n## Ethics and India\n- Organ donation rates in India are low (< 1 per million population), so awareness and counselling of families matter. Tamil Nadu and Kerala lead deceased donation programmes.\n- Doctors must communicate brain death clearly (\"death\") and separate the death declaration from the request for donation.","q":{"b":["Brain death = irreversible loss of all brain including brainstem.","Legal in India under THOTA 1994 (amended 2011).","Exclude drugs, hypothermia, metabolic causes, shock.","Absent pupillary, corneal, doll's-eye, caloric, gag, cough reflexes.","No motor response in cranial nerve territory.","Spinal reflexes may persist.","Apnoea test: no breathing at PaCO₂ ≥ 60 mmHg.","Board of 4 doctors; tests twice 6 h apart.","Transplant team excluded from the board.","EEG silence/absent blood flow – ancillary tests.","Diabetes insipidus and hypotension after brain death.","Vegetative state: brainstem intact.","Locked-in: conscious, ventral pons lesion."],"n":["Apnoea PaCO₂ ≥ 60 mmHg","Board of 4","Tests 6 h apart","Ice water 50 mL"],"m":"Brainstem reflexes **\"PCOV-GC + A\"**: Pupillary, Corneal, Oculocephalic, Vestibulo-ocular, Gag, Cough + Apnoea.","v":["Law governing brain death in India? THOTA 1994.","Endpoint PaCO₂ in the apnoea test? ≥ 60 mmHg.","Do spinal reflexes exclude brain death? No."]}},"ip|Free radicals & antioxidants":{"d":"## Definitions\nA **free radical** is an atom or molecule with one or more **unpaired electrons** in its outer orbit. That makes it highly reactive and short-lived, and it starts chain reactions.\n- **Reactive oxygen species (ROS)**:\n  - **superoxide (O₂•⁻)**\n  - **hydroxyl radical (•OH)**: the most reactive and damaging\n  - **hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂)**: not a radical but forms •OH\n  - singlet oxygen, hypochlorous acid (HOCl)\n- **Reactive nitrogen species (RNS)**: nitric oxide (NO•) and **peroxynitrite (ONOO⁻)** (NO + superoxide).\n- **Oxidative stress** is an imbalance where ROS/RNS production exceeds antioxidant defence.\n\n## Sources\n### Endogenous\n- **Mitochondrial electron transport chain**: ≈ 1–2% of O₂ leaks as superoxide (complexes I, III). The main source.\n- **Phagocytes**: the **respiratory (oxidative) burst** via **NADPH oxidase**, then myeloperoxidase (HOCl) in neutrophils, to kill bacteria. **Chronic granulomatous disease** is NADPH oxidase deficiency, with recurrent catalase-positive infections.\n- Xanthine oxidase (ischaemia–reperfusion), cytochrome P450, peroxisomes, NOS uncoupling.\n- **Fenton reaction**: Fe²⁺ + H₂O₂ → Fe³⁺ + •OH + OH⁻. **Haber–Weiss**: O₂•⁻ + H₂O₂ → •OH. Free iron (iron overload) is therefore dangerous.\n\n### Exogenous\n**Ionising radiation** (radiolysis of water), UV light, **smoking** (≈ 10¹⁵ radicals per puff), air pollution (relevant to Indian cities), drugs (paracetamol overdose: NAPQI; doxorubicin), pesticides (paraquat), alcohol, **hyperoxia** (O₂ therapy), heavy metals.\n\n## Damage\n- **Lipid peroxidation** of membrane polyunsaturated fatty acids: a chain reaction producing **malondialdehyde (MDA)**, a marker of oxidative stress (TBARS assay). Causes loss of membrane integrity.\n- **Protein oxidation**: carbonyls, cross-links, enzyme inactivation.\n- **DNA damage**: base oxidation (**8-hydroxy-2′-deoxyguanosine**, 8-OHdG, marker), strand breaks, mutation.\n\n## Physiological roles (not all bad)\n- Bacterial killing.\n- **Signalling**: H₂O₂ as a second messenger, NO in vasodilatation.\n- Thyroid hormone synthesis (H₂O₂ for TPO).\n- Sperm capacitation, insulin signalling, adaptation to exercise (hormesis).\n\n## Antioxidant defence\n### Enzymatic (primary defence)\n- **Superoxide dismutase (SOD)**: 2 O₂•⁻ + 2H⁺ → H₂O₂ + O₂.\n  - **Cu/Zn-SOD** (cytosol), **Mn-SOD** (mitochondria), extracellular SOD.\n  - SOD1 mutations cause familial ALS.\n- **Catalase** (peroxisomes, RBCs): 2 H₂O₂ → 2 H₂O + O₂. Contains haem iron.\n- **Glutathione peroxidase** (contains **selenium**): H₂O₂ + 2 GSH → 2 H₂O + GSSG.\n- **Glutathione reductase** regenerates GSH using **NADPH from the pentose phosphate pathway** (G6PD).\n  - **G6PD deficiency** (common in India): RBCs cannot regenerate GSH, so oxidant drugs (primaquine, dapsone, sulphonamides), fava beans and infections cause **haemolysis** with Heinz bodies.\n- Peroxiredoxins, thioredoxin.\n\n### Non-enzymatic\n- **Water-soluble**:\n  - **Vitamin C (ascorbate)**: scavenges radicals and **regenerates vitamin E**\n  - **glutathione (GSH)**: the main intracellular thiol antioxidant; **N-acetylcysteine replenishes it** in paracetamol poisoning\n  - **uric acid**: a major plasma antioxidant\n  - bilirubin, albumin\n- **Lipid-soluble**:\n  - **vitamin E (α-tocopherol)**: the main **chain-breaking antioxidant in membranes** (stops lipid peroxidation). Deficiency causes haemolysis in premature infants and neuropathy.\n  - **β-carotene**, lycopene, ubiquinone (CoQ10)\n- **Metal-binding** (prevent Fenton): transferrin, ferritin, lactoferrin, ceruloplasmin, haptoglobin.\n- **Dietary**: polyphenols and flavonoids (tea, fruit, turmeric/curcumin), trace elements (selenium, zinc, copper, manganese).\n\n## Disease associations\n- **Ischaemia–reperfusion injury** (myocardial infarction after thrombolysis, transplant, stroke): xanthine oxidase; the burst on reoxygenation.\n- **Atherosclerosis**: **oxidised LDL** is taken up by macrophage scavenger receptors, forming foam cells.\n- Ageing (free radical theory), cancer, diabetic complications, neurodegeneration (Parkinson's, Alzheimer's, ALS).\n- **Retinopathy of prematurity** and **bronchopulmonary dysplasia** (O₂ in neonates).\n- ARDS, cataract, rheumatoid arthritis, paracetamol hepatotoxicity, radiation injury.\n\n## Caution about supplements\n- Large trials of **antioxidant supplements** (vitamin E, β-carotene) have **not** shown protection against CVD or cancer.\n- **β-carotene increased lung cancer in smokers** (ATBC and CARET trials).\n- Antioxidants from a **diet rich in fruit and vegetables** are recommended over pills.","q":{"b":["Free radical = unpaired electron.","Hydroxyl radical – most reactive.","Mitochondria – main endogenous source.","NADPH oxidase – respiratory burst; CGD if deficient.","Fenton reaction: Fe²⁺ + H₂O₂ → •OH.","Lipid peroxidation marker – MDA.","SOD → H₂O₂; catalase/GPx remove H₂O₂.","Glutathione peroxidase contains selenium.","G6PD deficiency → ↓NADPH → oxidant haemolysis.","Vitamin E – chain-breaking membrane antioxidant.","Vitamin C regenerates vitamin E.","NAC replenishes glutathione (paracetamol).","Oxidised LDL → foam cells.","β-carotene supplements ↑ lung cancer in smokers."],"n":["≈ 1–2% O₂ → superoxide","Selenium in GPx","Cu/Zn & Mn SOD"],"m":"Antioxidant enzymes **\"SCG\"** – SOD, Catalase, Glutathione peroxidase (Se).","v":["Most reactive oxygen radical? Hydroxyl radical.","Trace element in glutathione peroxidase? Selenium.","Main lipid-soluble antioxidant? Vitamin E."]}},"ip|Space physiology":{"d":"## Environment\n- **Microgravity** (\"weightlessness\", ≈ 10⁻⁶ g in orbit, i.e. continuous free fall).\n- Ionising **radiation** (galactic cosmic rays, solar particle events; above the Earth's protective atmosphere, partly inside the magnetosphere for the ISS).\n- Isolation and confinement, altered light–dark cycles (≈ 16 sunrises per day in low Earth orbit), noise, vacuum outside the spacecraft.\n- Indian context: **Gaganyaan** human spaceflight programme (ISRO). Astronaut selection and training through the Indian Air Force **Institute of Aerospace Medicine, Bengaluru**. Indian-origin astronauts include Rakesh Sharma (1984) and Shubhanshu Shukla (Axiom-4, 2025).\n\n## Launch and re-entry\n- **Acceleration (G-forces)**: ≈ 3–4 G at launch (Shuttle ≈ 3 G), up to ≈ 4–8 G on re-entry (higher in ballistic re-entry).\n- **Positive Gz** (head-to-foot) pools blood in the legs, so **↓cerebral blood flow → grey-out (≈ 4 G), black-out (≈ 5 G), G-LOC (G-induced loss of consciousness)**.\n- Hence astronauts are seated **supine/transverse (Gx: chest-to-back)**, which is tolerated up to ≈ 15 G or more. Fighter pilots use **anti-G suits** and straining manoeuvres.\n- Negative Gz (foot-to-head) causes \"red-out\" and cerebral congestion.\n\n## Effects of microgravity\n### Fluid shift and cardiovascular\n- **Headward fluid shift** (≈ 1.5–2 L from the legs): \"**puffy face, bird legs**\", nasal congestion.\n- Central volume receptors (atria) read this as overload: **↓ADH, ↓aldosterone, ↑ANP** → **diuresis**, ↓plasma volume (≈ 10–15%).\n- **Space anaemia**: ↑RBC destruction (haemolysis) and ↓EPO; RBC mass falls ≈ 10–15%.\n- **Cardiac deconditioning**: ↓LV mass, ↓stroke volume, ↓VO₂max.\n- **On return: orthostatic intolerance** (≈ 20–60% of astronauts after short flights; more after long flights) from low blood volume, impaired baroreflex and venous pooling. Countermeasures: **fluid and salt loading before re-entry**, anti-G garments, midodrine, exercise.\n\n### Musculoskeletal\n- **Bone loss ≈ 1–1.5% per month** in weight-bearing bones (hip, spine) from unloading: ↑osteoclastic resorption, ↓formation. Causes **hypercalciuria → risk of renal stones**. Recovery after return is slow and incomplete.\n- **Muscle atrophy**: especially antigravity muscles (soleus, back extensors), type I → II shift; ↓strength by up to 20% or more in long flights.\n- **Spinal lengthening** (≈ 3–5 cm; disc expansion) causes back pain.\n- Countermeasures: **daily ≈ 2 h exercise** (treadmill with harness, cycle ergometer, advanced resistive exercise device), bisphosphonates, adequate vitamin D/calcium (no sunlight).\n\n### Neurovestibular\n- **Space motion sickness (space adaptation syndrome)** in ≈ 60–70% in the first 2–3 days. Sensory conflict: otoliths no longer signal \"down\".\n- Spatial disorientation, illusions; re-adaptation on return (gait ataxia, \"Mal de débarquement\").\n\n### Eyes\n**Spaceflight-associated neuro-ocular syndrome (SANS)**: optic disc oedema, globe flattening, choroidal folds, hyperopic shift; from the headward fluid shift and altered intracranial pressure. Seen in long-duration crews.\n\n### Others\n- **Immune dysregulation** (↓T-cell function, latent virus reactivation such as herpes).\n- **Sleep disruption** (circadian misalignment: melatonin, light management).\n- **Psychological stress**, ↓appetite and weight loss, ↓plasma volume.\n- **Radiation risks**: cancer, cataract, CNS effects; shielding and dosimetry (limits are a career dose).\n- Altered drug pharmacokinetics; microbiome changes.\n- The NASA Twins Study (2019) showed most changes reverse after return, though some (telomere changes, some gene expression) persist.\n\n## Ground-based analogues\n**6° head-down tilt bed rest**, dry immersion, parabolic flights (≈ 20–25 s of weightlessness each).\n\n## Life support\nCabin pressure ≈ 1 atm (ISS, 21% O₂) or reduced pressure with higher O₂ (spacesuits ≈ 0.3 atm of 100% O₂, so **pre-breathing O₂ to wash out nitrogen** prevents decompression sickness before EVA). CO₂ scrubbing (lithium hydroxide, amine systems), water recycling.","q":{"b":["Microgravity – continuous free fall.","Positive Gz → grey-out (4 G), black-out (5 G), G-LOC.","Astronauts seated supine (Gx) – tolerate higher G.","Headward fluid shift ≈ 1.5–2 L: puffy face, bird legs.","↓ADH, ↑ANP → diuresis; plasma volume ↓.","Space anaemia – RBC mass ↓.","Orthostatic intolerance on return – fluid loading.","Bone loss ≈ 1–1.5% per month; renal stone risk.","Antigravity muscle atrophy; daily ≈ 2 h exercise.","Space motion sickness in first 2–3 days.","SANS – optic disc oedema, hyperopic shift.","Pre-breathe O₂ before EVA to prevent DCS.","Head-down tilt bed rest – ground analogue."],"n":["Bone loss 1–1.5%/month","Fluid shift 1.5–2 L","Black-out ≈ 5 G","Head-down tilt 6°"],"m":"Microgravity **\"Puffy face, bird legs, brittle bones\"**.","v":["Main cardiovascular problem after return from space? Orthostatic intolerance.","Why is ADH reduced in space? Headward fluid shift stimulates atrial volume receptors.","Rate of bone loss in space? About 1–1.5% per month."]}}});